# Daily trace prune. Landed from the host 2026-07-29 alongside waterevents-janitor.service — see that file for why it
# was running in production without existing in any committed file.
#
# Persistent=true so a host that was powered off over the scheduled time prunes on the next boot instead of skipping a
# run. Trace growth is monotonic, so a missed run is a permanent step up in disk use, not a gap that heals.
#
# HOURLY, NOT DAILY, because the retention number has to mean something. The fleet writes traces at a measured
# ~1,565/hour: one hour after a successful prune to 500, the tree was already back to 2,065. On a daily timer it
# therefore spends almost all of its time somewhere around 37,000 directories and ~11GB, and "keep the newest 500" is
# a figure that is true for a few seconds a day. Hourly keeps it near 2,000, which is what the setting says.
# It is also cheap at that size: pruning 127,451 directories took about three minutes, so pruning ~1,500 is seconds.
# {MEASURED 2026-08-01 traces 500 -> 2065 in the hour after the janitor ran}
# {MEASURED 2026-08-01 "janitor: 127573 -> 523 directories (removed 127050)" — what a day of unbounded growth cost}
# [CONFIDENCE: CONFIRMED 100% — both counts read off the production host an hour apart.]
[Unit]
Description=Prune WaterEvents crawl traces hourly

[Timer]
OnCalendar=hourly
Persistent=true

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
